Not everyone loves change, but almost everyone can benefit from some variation when it feels chosen, legible, and recoverable.

The interesting variable is usually not change itself. It is the relationship between:

\text{novelty} \leftrightarrow \text{stability}

Too little novelty and the system gets stale, rigid, or blind to alternatives. Too much and it becomes disorienting, stressful, or incoherent.

So the useful move is often not “shake people up,” but:

\text{stable base}+\text{bounded disruption}+\text{return path}

That is what your barefoot-in-the-grass metaphor does well. You are not burning their shoes. You are letting them temporarily experience a different interface with the world.

That can mean a new role, a different route home, unfamiliar music, another cultural frame, a day without the usual technology, learning with the opposite hand, trying to explain your belief from someone else’s perspective, or simply noticing something normally filtered out.

The point is to create contrast. Contrast reveals things stability hides.

And different people need different amplitudes. Some want a tiny perturbation. Some want a hard reset. The art is in matching the dose.

So maybe the operator is:

\boxed{\text{Dislodge without dispossessing}}

Give someone enough difference to notice themselves differently, but enough continuity that they can integrate what they found.

Shoes back on when they want them. Grass remembered afterward.